# Migrating to role bindings

Control plane v0.172.0 moved access control out of the resources. Who may use
a resource used to be stored inside it: the `collaborators` of a provider
account, MCP server, agent, ML repo, or secret group, and the `permissions` of
a virtual account. Access is now a separate **role binding**, a grant of the
form "this subject has this role on this resource".

TrueFoundry still accepts the old fields until **15 Nov 2026**. After that
date, any apply that still sends them fails. Existing grants are kept either way.

| Deprecated attribute | Resources |
| --- | --- |
| `collaborators` | `truefoundry_custom_endpoint_account`, `truefoundry_guardrail_group`, `truefoundry_mcp_server`, `truefoundry_openapi_mcp_server`, `truefoundry_virtual_mcp_server`, `truefoundry_agent`, `truefoundry_remote_agent`, `truefoundry_ml_repo`, `truefoundry_secret_group` |
| `permissions` | `truefoundry_virtual_account` |

Their replacement is [`truefoundry_role_binding`](../resources/role_binding.md).

## How the deprecated attributes behave

| In the configuration | Sent to the control plane | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| attribute removed | nothing | existing grants stay; Terraform stops tracking them |
| non-empty set | the list | grants are replaced |
| `collaborators = []` | an empty list | grants are cleared (accepted until 15 Nov 2026) |

Removing the attribute is safe. Nobody loses access, and later plans don't
report the remaining grants as drift. `permissions = []` is not allowed: the
control plane rejects an empty list for virtual accounts.

## Field mapping

A role binding has either **one subject with many permissions** or **many
subjects with one permission**. Never many of both.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| `collaborators[*].subject = "user:jane@example.com"` | `subjects = [{ type = "user", name = "jane@example.com" }]` |
| `collaborators[*].subject = "team:everyone"` | `subjects = [{ type = "team", name = "everyone" }]` |
| `collaborators[*].role_id` | `permissions[*].role` |
| the resource carrying the collaborators | `permissions[*].resource_type` and `permissions[*].resource_fqn` (its `fqn` attribute) |
| virtual account `permissions[*].resource_fqn` / `resource_type` / `role_id` | `permissions[*].resource_fqn` / `resource_type` / `role` |
| the virtual account carrying the permissions | `subjects = [{ type = "virtualaccount", name = "<account name>" }]` |

Collaborators become **one binding per role**: all subjects with the same role
share a binding. A virtual account's permissions become **one binding for the
account** that holds all its permissions.

## Porting collaborators

```hcl
resource "truefoundry_custom_endpoint_account" "speech" {
  name = "speech"
  # ...

  collaborators = [
    { subject = "user:jane@example.com", role_id = "provider-account-manager" },
    { subject = "team:everyone", role_id = "provider-account-access" },
  ]
}
```

becomes:

```hcl
resource "truefoundry_custom_endpoint_account" "speech" {
  name = "speech"
  # ...
}

resource "truefoundry_role_binding" "speech_managers" {
  name     = "speech-managers"
  subjects = [{ type = "user", name = "jane@example.com" }]
  permissions = [{
    resource_type = "provider-account"
    resource_fqn  = truefoundry_custom_endpoint_account.speech.fqn
    role          = "provider-account-manager"
  }]
}

resource "truefoundry_role_binding" "speech_users" {
  name     = "speech-users"
  subjects = [{ type = "team", name = "everyone" }]
  permissions = [{
    resource_type = "provider-account"
    resource_fqn  = truefoundry_custom_endpoint_account.speech.fqn
    role          = "provider-account-access"
  }]
}
```

## Porting virtual account permissions

```hcl
resource "truefoundry_virtual_account" "ci" {
  name = "ci-bot"
  permissions = [
    { resource_type = "provider-account", resource_fqn = "my-tenant:azure-openai:gpt", role_id = "provider-account-access" },
  ]
}
```

becomes:

```hcl
resource "truefoundry_virtual_account" "ci" {
  name = "ci-bot"
}

resource "truefoundry_role_binding" "ci" {
  name     = "va-ci-bot"
  subjects = [{ type = "virtualaccount", name = truefoundry_virtual_account.ci.name }]
  permissions = [
    { resource_type = "provider-account", resource_fqn = "my-tenant:azure-openai:gpt", role = "provider-account-access" },
  ]
}
```

## Rollout

1. Add the `truefoundry_role_binding` resources and remove the deprecated
   attributes in the same change, then apply. The old grants stay in place,
   so access never drops.
2. Check the resource's **Access Control** page in the TrueFoundry UI: every
   grant should now appear through a role binding.
3. Optional: to also remove the old grants that Terraform no longer tracks,
   set `collaborators = []` on the resource, apply, then remove the attribute.
   Do this before 15 Nov 2026, because the control plane rejects the empty list after that date.
   Virtual account permissions can only be revoked in the UI.

